\section{Keep the Abstract Tree and the Lossless Tree Distinct}

An optimizer may want \texttt{Add(Name(x), Int(1))}, which says
nothing about bytes separating the expressions or the parentheses
around the addition. A refactoring tool needs the source order,
comments, delimiters, and even unexpected tokens. Syntax trees as
representations of operators and operands are standard compiler
practice \cite{aho2007compilers}; the lowering from a lossless syntax
tree to such an abstract syntax tree should be explicit. For
example, an AST can erase parentheses while retaining a source
occurrence on the result for a diagnostic. It should not treat
the surviving inner expression as the exact range to replace
when performing a source edit that must also remove parentheses.

One useful lossless representation is a green tree of immutable
nodes and tokens. A green node has a kind, ordered children,
and total byte width. It has no parent and no absolute start.
Token leaves own or refer to immutable bytes whose lifetime is at
least that of the green node, and every real byte contributes its
width exactly once, including trivia. The widths use the same
byte unit as the snapshot spans in \Cref{ch:ext-421}.
A synthetic missing token
has width zero and a distinct kind or flag; discarded input is
retained as error text instead of disappearing. A red view wraps
a green node with the root revision and occurrence context,
typically a parent and child index or an absolute start reached
by accumulating the widths of preceding siblings.

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{lll}
\hline
Object & Suitable key & Information deliberately omitted \\
\hline
Green node & Arena ID or structural key & File, parent, absolute position \\
Red occurrence & Root revision and child path & Independently copied children \\
Lowered AST node & Lowering run and AST ID & Trivia and syntax-only grouping \\
Semantic result & Occurrence and enclosing context & Physical green allocation site \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\section{Share Shape Without Sharing Location}

Suppose two distinct statement occurrences contain a child
fragment \texttt{x+1}. They could both point to the same green
node, as in \Cref{fig:visual-422}. Their red starts differ and
they may bind \texttt{x} to different declarations. Even in
one revision, using a green pointer as the key for a resolved
name cache is not sound. Byte width and structural hash are
properties of the shape and can be cached there; resolved type
and absolute span are properties of an occurrence or its
semantic context. The useful abstraction is a tree of
occurrences implemented by a directed acyclic graph of immutable
objects, not a graph of uniquely positioned children.

