\section{Rebuild the Changed Path After Parsing}

\begin{verbatim}
red_child(view, index):
    require 0 <= index < view.green.child_count
    prefix := view.green.width_before_child(index)
    return Red(view.revision, view, index,
               view.start + prefix, view.green.child[index])

splice(root, path, reparsed):
    # Caller has certified a grammar-compatible boundary,
    # including the effects of lexical and parse context.
    if path.is_empty(): return reparsed
    i := path.first
    old := root.child[i]
    new := splice(old, path.rest, reparsed)
    if new is old: return root
    children := root.children.copy()
    children[i] := new
    return make_green(root.kind, children)

make_green(kind, children):
    width := checked_sum(child.width for child in children)
    return Green(kind, freeze(children), width)
\end{verbatim}

The replacement must come from the parser, not directly from
substitution of an arbitrary text fragment. Replacing \texttt{x}
in \texttt{x*y} by \texttt{a+b} without parentheses may
require rebuilding the multiplication context because the
expected tree is now an addition. Diekmann's incremental parsing
account asks not just whether a subtree's text survived an edit
but whether the parse state and context allow it to be reused.
A simple compiler can reparse the entire file, then reuse
exactly matching green objects. A faster parser may certify
smaller boundaries; the green layout alone certifies none.

When the edit lengthens one of the two fragments in the figure,
untouched right siblings can keep their green objects. Their red
views must be created from the new root and may report larger
starts. Conversely, deleting and reinserting identical text can
yield the same final green shape without preserving editor
bookmarks that were attached to the deleted occurrence. A
bookmark needs a document edit mapping, not an equality query
on the new tree. If red views are memoized, store them by
occurrence and revision, or use a bounded cache; retaining a red
parent chain can keep an entire old revision reachable.

\section{Bound the Cost of Wide Nodes and Canonicalization}

Path copying has low cost for many fixed-arity expressions but
\texttt{children.copy()} is linear in arity. Editing one
declaration in a flat list of one hundred thousand declarations
is not a logarithmic operation. Segment wide child lists into
a persistent sequence, with aggregate widths and counts for
subtrees, if traces show these edits dominate. For small nodes,
a compact consecutive vector plus a linear prefix scan may
be best. If profiling shows repeated random child lookup in
moderately wide nodes, a separate prefix-width array makes
individual lookups cheap while adding update and memory costs.
Use a checked offset type common to the source buffer and green
widths so that malformed or artificially huge input cannot
wrap the prefix sum.

Hash-consing, which looks up an immutable node by its structural
key before allocating, is optional. Reusing existing subtrees
from the previous tree does not require canonical identity for
all equal fragments across the workspace. A structural hash
must be verified against kind and child sequence on collision.
If the table strongly owns all interned green nodes, it also
prevents reclaiming history after editors discard their roots.
Limit interning to selected immutable leaves or use weak
entries with a reclamation policy when retaining nodes costs
more than repeat construction.

\section{Check the Tree at Both Consumer Boundaries}

For the lossless consumer, concatenate original token bytes in
occurrence order; the result must equal the source snapshot.
Assert that each child's prefix and width fit inside its parent,
including missing tokens sharing a location with real tokens.
For the abstract consumer, compare the lowered tree after a
scripted edit to a clean parse and lowering of the edited text.
Do not compare allocation IDs; compare operators, ordered
operands, and occurrence spans in the new revision. Keep a version
of the old root live during these checks to detect accidental
mutation through shared objects.

One discriminating fixture contains two identical functions in
different namespaces, with a comment in the first that becomes
longer. Check that a reused fragment in the second function
reports its shifted range and still names the second namespace's
declaration. Run the fixture with green interning both enabled
and disabled. A difference in name resolution is evidence that
some semantic key is really encoding allocation or shape rather
than a source occurrence.

\section{Build Green Nodes From a Parser Event Stream}

A top-down parser sometimes recognizes a prefix before it knows
which node will enclose it. For \texttt{x + 1 * 2}, it can
first recognize a name and then see the addition operator.
Rather than allocating and mutating a node to add a parent,
record an event stream and use a builder to create complete
immutable nodes. The parser can open a marker before parsing
the left operand and classify that marker once it sees the
operator. An unused marker is ignored at build time. The
builder makes tokens children of the innermost active marker,
in source order. A lexer that feeds significant tokens to the
parser should also tell the builder which trivia must be
inserted around them.

\begin{verbatim}
Start(Add)
    Start(Name); Token("x"); Finish
    Token(" "); Token("+"); Token(" ")
    Start(Multiply)
        Token("1"); Token(" "); Token("*"); Token(" "); Token("2")
    Finish
Finish
\end{verbatim}

Omit the final \texttt{2} and a recovery event should produce
a missing operand without inventing bytes. Materialize a
zero-width missing node with the expected role; preserve
the existing space and operator. If a later input supplies
the operand, an unchanged prefix can be reused regardless of
whether error nodes are interned. Test that a traversal that
filters out missing nodes reports no extra text, while
one that gathers completions still has a precise empty
range at which to suggest an operand.
